  "about": "Second-device linking (spec §3, #96). Each case pins the seed s and the one-time X25519 secrets e_A and e_B, and expects their public keys, the commitment SHA-256(E_A), the pairing mailbox id's bytes (HKDF(s, empty salt, 'reconcile/v1/link-mailbox', 32); the id is their unpadded base64url) and seal key (HKDF(s, empty salt, 'reconcile/v1/link-seal', 32)), ikm = X25519(e_A, E_B) = X25519(e_B, E_A), T = UTF-8 'reconcile/v1/link' ‖ s ‖ E_A ‖ E_B, the number u32be(HKDF(ikm, SHA-256(T), 'reconcile/v1/link-number', 4)) mod 10^6 as six digits, K = HKDF(ikm, SHA-256(T), 'reconcile/v1/link-key', 32), and the bundle mailbox id's bytes (HKDF(K, empty salt, 'reconcile/v1/link-bundle', 32)). HKDF is HKDF-SHA-256 (RFC 5869). Computed with Python's hmac and hashlib, each HKDF checked against the cryptography package's, and X25519 by the cryptography package (OpenSSL), which also refused every low-order point listed. Neither is @noble. Bytes are lists of numbers. The code is 'RL1.' and then the unpadded base64url of s ‖ the commitment. Mailbox ids are kept as bytes and the code isn't written out, so no high-entropy text sits in the repository.",
